l_stats.json cases.F1.studied_percent_of_mean_participant_comparisons=1.1885 with mean_comparisons_per_participant=5216.5; claims_grounding.json claim on 2N/M (5,217 and 860; 1.2% and 7.2%; mid 0.077% and 0.93%).\n  - requested_fix: Remove the claims that the direction reverses and that the views disagree about which table looks more fragile. State that sports has the larger budget share under both normalisers, with a smaller ratio under the per-participant view (about 6x top and 12x mid versus 10.5x and 21.0x). Keep the confound caveat.\n- `qualified-means-controls-passed` (Experimental Protocol 4.3, Discussion (iii), Supplement Controls and qualification details): final.qualification.* grounds only outcome=qualified, status=completed, correct=true, and candidate diagnostics unverified. No artifact records which gates or criteria the qualification decision covers or that it certifies controls. Reading qualified as certifying controls and validity is an unsupported inference. The recorded control outcomes (empty-set no flip, non-vacuous permutation, copy 0 of 2) are separate observations from final.evidence.episodes.\n  - claim: We read this as: the evaluation ran to completion and its controls passed. The qualified outcome of the held-out evaluation certifies that the evaluation completed with its controls passing.\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation (outcome, status, correct only; no gate diagnostics); claims_grounding.json claim on qualification (outcome qualified, status completed, success indicator true, diagnostics unverified); paper.tex lines for the qualification subsection and Discussion (iii).\n  - requested_fix: Say only that the sealed evaluation completed and was recorded as qualified with a true success indicator. Report the control results as separate observations from the episode evidence. Delete the statements that qualified certifies controls passing, and add that the qualification gate criteria are not recorded in the evidence.\n- `evaluator-internals-unsupported` (Problem Setup 3.4 and 3.5, Supplement Controls and Algorithmic specification and Data provenance): These describe evaluator internals (comparator construction, control thresholds, acceptance rule, abort-on-overlap and checksum behaviour, copy-control mechanics). The only inspectable source is the candidate and baseline program under source_evidence/downstream, and no evaluator source or spec is staged. claims_grounding.json has no claim for these details. The abort-on-overlap statement also sits awkwardly beside the recorded removal of 2 overlap rows and the 652 kept prompt-only overlaps.\n  - claim: B3 deletes rows where the upper participant beat another participant, ordered by opponent rating. The permutation control is non-vacuous only within 90% of expected moved fraction and spread at most half. The evaluator's acceptance test is theta_hi<theta_lo with no margin. Held-out content hashes are tested for overlap and the evaluation aborts on any overlap; tables are checked by checksum. Cross-table copy rescales row indices.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/downstream contains only baseline and program (program.py, datasets_offline.py, program_spec.toml); claims_grounding.json covers budgets, scores and control outcomes but not comparator or control construction; derived/task_meta/final_meta.json shows 2 rows removed and 652 prompt-only overlaps kept.\n  - requested_fix: Remove or explicitly mark as not independently verifiable the evaluator-implementation specifics: the B3 construction, the 90% and half-spread thresholds, the no-margin acceptance rule, abort-on-overlap, checksum verification, and the copy-control rescaling. Keep only the observed control outcomes that final.evidence.episodes records.\n- `dev-breakdown-grounding-mismatch` (Results: Development tables, Table dev, Supplement Development tables additional detail): The claims_grounding.json entries cite result.best_correct_score and result.top_program_01 to 03, which each carry only the 0.4993 combined score. paper_data.json does not contain the per-family, flip-rate, random-reference, budget-fraction or control-gap breakdown for the search scope, and the sub-scores appear only in generated prompt text. Descriptions of configurations 2 and 3 rest on idea titles, with no inspectable source.\n  - claim: Crowd 0.9987, expert 0.0, flip rate 0.5, random reference 0.0695, influence reference 0.4993, mean budget fraction 0.5007, control gap 0.3153 for each of the three configurations; configurations 2 and 3 described as cell-aggregated relaxed search with exact swap polishing and a stationarity LP.\n  - evidence: paper_data.json grounding_sources result.top_program_01 to 03 (value 0.4993478 only); all_programs entries (combined_score and durations only); claims_grounding.json development claim and its source_ids; source_evidence/downstream has no source for configurations 2 and 3.\n  - requested_fix: Either anchor the development sub-metrics to a resolvable evidence source or state that they are quoted from the search-stage evaluator output without a registry entry. Limit the Table dev columns for configurations 2 and 3 to the grounded score and duration. Describe those two configurations only at title level, with no claim about their algorithms.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval targets.\n\nCoverage of Bradley–Terry theory (extensions, existence/consistency, uncertainty) is dense. The existing bibliography does not cover three areas the draft relies on or motivates itself with. The draft concedes this in its \"Scope of the bibliography\" paragraph and in the Extended limitations. Numeric claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not targets.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Chatbot Arena / MT-Bench pairwise LLM evaluation and the datasets used\n- Influence functions and leave-one-out / Cook's-distance-style deletion diagnostics\n- Manipulation and rigging of crowd-vote or pairwise-comparison rankings\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **arena-leaderboard-context** (Introduction, high, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"Leaderboards that aggregate 'which of A and B is better' judgments into Bradley–Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models…\"\n   - Reason: The motivating claim about leaderboard use has no citation. The Data section uses Arena 55k/140k and MT-Bench human judgments without citing the platform or dataset papers.\n   - Search topics: Chatbot Arena platform paper; MT-Bench human judgments / LLM-as-a-judge; Arena human-preference dataset.\n\n2. **influence-deletion-method** (Problem Setup and Method, high, background)\n   - Anchor: \"Deleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap…\"\n   - Reason: The first-order influence formula with leverage correction is the core of the studied configuration and the basis of the B2 comparator. It has no citation to influence-function or case-deletion literature. Only Broderick et al. is cited.\n   - Search topics: influence functions for black-box predictions; Cook's distance / case deletion in logistic regression; one-step leave-one-out approximations for M-estimators.\n\n3. **manipulation-positioning** (Related Work, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"Work on adversarial manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards, on evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on influence functions…\"\n   - Reason: The draft motivates the removal budget through deliberate action and rigging, then disclaims any positioning against that work. One or two citations would replace the disclaimer.\n   - Search topics: adversarial vote manipulation of LLM arena leaderboards; robustness of pairwise-comparison rankings to corrupted comparisons.\n\n## Not targeted\nRelated-work paragraphs on BT extensions, estimation and uncertainty are well supported. Adding more there would only grow the bibliography.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy: refit-verified removal budgets for Bradley-Terry leaderboards\n\nStatus: ready. Prompt family: `paper_standalone` (skill `write-paper-standalone`). Venue: ICLR 2026, single-column, 5.5 in text width, 8 to 9 body pages, double-blind, LLM-usage section required. Edit mode is not active (no prior manuscript staged), so there is no prior story to preserve or recenter.\n\n## Grounded state of the run\n- Search stage: three scored attempts (0 unscored), all at search score 0.4993, equal to the starting baseline (delta 0.0). Investigation outcome `completed_no_winner`; the frozen selection is a non-winner candidate (source attempt c01). Search data: arena-55k (64 models, 57,477 rows) and MT-Bench human judgments (6 models, 3,355 rows). Crowd family 0.9987; expert family 0.0; flip rate 0.5.\n- Sealed final stage (separate scope, different data): outcome **qualified**, status completed. Data: arena-140k after content-hash deduplication (52 models, 135,630 rows) and NBA 2006 to 2015 (30 teams, 12,900 rows). Mean score 0.926 (crowd 0.988, sports 0.864); random-removal reference 0.041; influence-only reference 0.926; flip rate 1.0; control gap +0.112.\n- Per-case verified budgets (rows; cap = ceil(2% N)): crowd top 62 (cap 2,713), crowd mid 4, sports top 62 (cap 258), sports mid 8. Influence-only B2 gives the identical 62, 4, 62, 8; opponent-targeted B3 gives 650, 6, 88, 12; random B1 flipped 0, 6, 0, 0 of 20 draws. Permutation controls flip with 2 and 10 rows and are non-vacuous; empty set never flips; copy control 0 of 2. Both final episodes were identical.\n\n## Central question\nUnder an exact Bradley-Terry refit (ridge 0.01, ties as half wins), what is the smallest set of comparisons within a 2% cap whose deletion reverses an adjacent top or mid pair, and does that budget differ between a crowd-vote table and a sports table under one verified protocol?\n\n## Central thesis\nAdjacent-pair reversals need very few deleted comparisons (4 to 62 of 135,630 crowd rows, about 0.003% to 0.046%; 8 to 62 of 12,900 NBA rows, about 0.06% to 0.48%, derived as k/N), mid pairs are cheaper than top pairs, and the sports budget is about ten times larger as a share of comparisons. But first-order influence ordering with exact-refit bisection already reaches every measured budget, so the elaborate search stages add nothing measurable, and the design contrast is confounded with table size, participant count and era. The claim is a bounded descriptive one, not a method-superiority or causal claim.\n\n## Why this framing\nA method-superiority story is unsupported (selected equals B2 everywhere; search delta zero). A run-diary of three tied variants is not a paper. A measurement study with the search procedure as an instrument, evaluator-owned baselines and controls, and honest scope is supported and acceptable under the task (\"small sensitivity\", \"depends on conditions\" are valid outcomes). Qualified is reported as achieved but only certifies that the evaluation completed with controls passing. Expert-judgment evidence exists only from the search stage (no flip within the cap on the 6-model table); its cause (myopic search vs infeasibility) is unknown and must be labelled so.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Studied configuration (proposed, as instrument): influence-on-gap with leverage correction, re-linearised greedy, prefix bisection, drop-and-readd polish, exact-refit acceptance. Budget is an upper bound on the minimum.\n2. B1 random removal (baseline), B2 influence-only (baseline, decisive comparator), B3 opponent-targeted (baseline).\n3. Validity controls (supporting): empty set, label permutation, cross-table copy.\n4. Search-stage variants (supporting, compact): cell-aggregated relaxed search and stationarity LP tied at 0.4993; runtimes about 1.3 s, 2.0 s, 114 s per episode. Presented as a negative result, not chronology.\nThe starting baseline program was not run in the final stage, so no final-stage selected-vs-starting comparison is written; the search-stage tie is reported as such.\n\n## Results views\nFinal-stage per-case budgets with comparators; design contrast with explicit confounds; top vs mid; controls; the studied-equals-B2 negative result; search-stage tie and expert non-flip kept in a separate section; scope statement (point-estimate order reversal of one adjacent pair, 2% cap, no CI events, no rigging or insertion, no model-judge design, two final designs, two identical episodes, no significance claims).\n\n## Figure specs (all full text width in the single-column template)\n1. `protocol_overview` (diagram, `generate-concept-figure`, full width 1.0, early placement page 1 or 2): protocol pipeline, baselines and controls, designs and stages. 1 panel.\n2. `main_budget_by_case` (grouped bar, matplotlib, full width 1.0): 2 panels (crowd, sports), 2 categories (top, mid), 3 series (studied, B2, B3); log y, percent of comparisons, cap line; random flips annotated as x/20; studied and B2 visually distinguishable because they coincide.\n3. `controls_permutation` (paired bar, matplotlib, width 0.9): real vs permuted budget; spread ratio. Convert to a table if it will not read at size; do not shrink.\nExport PDF plus 300 DPI PNG, reference the PDF; raster-only needs at least 2400 px at full width and must be regenerated, not upscaled. Tables carry the dataset summary, per-case results and search-variant results.\n\n## Body-page budget (8 to 9 pages, last page at least 75% full)\nIntroduction 1.0; related work 0.6; problem and method 2.0 (with Figure 1); protocol and datasets 1.0; results 2.5 (Figures 2 and 3, tables, search tie, expert non-flip); discussion and limitations 1.0. Extra room goes to sharper definitions and limitation analysis, not to extra claims.\n\n## Supplementary plan\nFull per-case and per-episode table; controls and qualification details; search-variant table with unknown expert-table cause; algorithm and protocol specification; data provenance, dedup counts and reproduction; LLM-usage disclosure and extended limitations. Nothing essential to the thesis is moved there.\n\n## Forbidden\nHeadings: Experiment 1/2, Campaign Overview, Search Progression, Milestones, First Valid Result, Iteration History, Attempt Chronology, What We Tried, Lessons Learned from the Run, Pipeline Details. Phrases: correctness rate, correct and eligible, eligible, combined_score, rubric, worker names, \"state-of-the-art\", \"outperforms the baseline\", \"significantly better\", \"proves the minimum\", \"design X causes\", chronology wording.\n\n## Writer notes\n- Report budgets in rows and percent of comparisons; t","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/agent-home/.claude/projects/-workspace-paper/35d0884b-633b-4c96-8540-7a451c3c9e1e/tool-results/b0zrc9ph9.txt","persistedOutputSize":30498}}
