{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgvps5gww7bK5nCCRsnYT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016KiAunS3dbtxx3gvqbbbzH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; cp paper.tex paper.tex.bak_before_audit; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, old[:60]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n\n# 3.3 comparators/controls\na=s.index('Three comparators are computed by the evaluator')\nb=s.index('\\\\subsection{Why these definitions}')\nnew='''The evaluator reports three comparators for each case, scored with the same rule as the studied configuration: \\\\textbf{B1}, random removal (20 random orders per case; we report how many flip within the cap); \\\\textbf{B2}, an influence-only removal, which the evaluator exposes as its ``influence reference''; and \\\\textbf{B3}, an opponent-targeted removal. The evaluator source is not part of the staged evidence, so we do not independently verify how B1--B3 are constructed. We rely on their recorded outcomes (flip counts and budgets) and on the names under which they are recorded. Our reading of B2 as one static first-order influence ranking followed by exact-refit prefix bisection, i.e.\\\\ step (a) below without leverage correction, re-linearisation or polishing, is consistent with the inspected source of the starting baseline (Section~\\\\ref{sec:dev}) but is an interpretation, not a verified fact. B3 is described by the evaluator only as an opponent-targeted removal, and we treat it as a hand-built comparator that needs no gradient.\n\nThree controls are recorded alongside the cases: an \\\\emph{empty removal set}, which should not flip any case; a \\\\emph{label-permutation} control on the top pair of each table, for which the evaluator records the fraction of moved labels against its expectation, the rating-spread ratio relative to the real fit, and a non-vacuity indicator; and a \\\\emph{cross-table copy} control, for which it records the number of flips over its trials. We report only these recorded outcomes. The evidence does not state the thresholds or construction details behind the non-vacuity indicator or the copy control, and we make no claim about them.\n\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"sec:dev\" paper.tex | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3436,"cache_read_input_tokens":74133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3436},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35d0884b-633b-4c96-8540-7a451c3c9e1e","uuid":"e5c43998-3859-4a00-9703-cccc55ee55fc","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:29:11.054Z","request_id":"req_011Cgvps4eRx3W7VhYczcvfw"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016KiAunS3dbtxx3gvqbbbzH","type":"tool_result","content":"91:The evaluator reports three comparators for each case, scored with the same rule as the studied configuration: \\textbf{B1}, random removal (20 random orders per case; we report how many flip within the cap); \\textbf{B2}, an influence-only removal, which the evaluator exposes as its ``influence reference''; and \\textbf{B3}, an opponent-targeted removal. The evaluator source is not part of the staged evidence, so we do not independently verify how B1--B3 are constructed. We rely on their recorded outcomes (flip counts and budgets) and on the names under which they are recorded. Our reading of B2 as one static first-order influence ranking followed by exact-refit prefix bisection, i.e.\\ step (a) below without leverage correction, re-linearisation or polishing, is consistent with the inspected source of the starting baseline (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}) but is an interpretation, not a verified fact. B3 is described by the evaluator only as an opponent-targeted removal, and we treat it as a hand-built comparator that needs no gradient.\n127:Search configurations were scored on the development tables, and one frozen configuration was then evaluated in a single sealed held-out evaluation. We keep the two scopes in separate tables and sections and never combine their scores. No search configuration exceeded the starting baseline on the development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}), so the frozen selection is not a winner; it is one of three configurations with identical development scores. Its held-out evaluation completed and was recorded as qualified. We read this as: the evaluation ran to completion and its controls passed. It is not evidence of superiority over the starting baseline, which was not run on the held-out tables, and it does not extend the findings beyond the declared cases.\n199:So the re-linearisation, the leverage correction and the polish gave no measured reduction on these cases. The same holds on development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}). A single static ranking by first-order influence followed by exact-refit bisection is therefore enough to reach every budget we report, and the budgets can be viewed as properties of that simple procedure's output rather than of the more elaborate one. Opponent-targeted removal needs more rows in all four cases: 650 versus 62, 6 versus 4, 88 versus 62 and 12 versus 8. The gap is large only for the crowd top pair (10.5 times), and modest elsewhere (1.4 to 1.5 times). Random removal's mean overall score is 0.041, against 0.926 for B2. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n215:\\subsection{Development tables: a three-way tie and an unflipped expert table}\\label{sec:dev}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35d0884b-633b-4c96-8540-7a451c3c9e1e","uuid":"191c1acf-41d0-4594-b196-60ed1d5600a7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:29:11.126Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"91:The evaluator reports three comparators for each case, scored with the same rule as the studied configuration: \\textbf{B1}, random removal (20 random orders per case; we report how many flip within the cap); \\textbf{B2}, an influence-only removal, which the evaluator exposes as its ``influence reference''; and \\textbf{B3}, an opponent-targeted removal. The evaluator source is not part of the staged evidence, so we do not independently verify how B1--B3 are constructed. We rely on their recorded outcomes (flip counts and budgets) and on the names under which they are recorded. Our reading of B2 as one static first-order influence ranking followed by exact-refit prefix bisection, i.e.\\ step (a) below without leverage correction, re-linearisation or polishing, is consistent with the inspected source of the starting baseline (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}) but is an interpretation, not a verified fact. B3 is described by the evaluator only as an opponent-targeted removal, and we treat it as a hand-built comparator that needs no gradient.\n127:Search configurations were scored on the development tables, and one frozen configuration was then evaluated in a single sealed held-out evaluation. We keep the two scopes in separate tables and sections and never combine their scores. No search configuration exceeded the starting baseline on the development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}), so the frozen selection is not a winner; it is one of three configurations with identical development scores. Its held-out evaluation completed and was recorded as qualified. We read this as: the evaluation ran to completion and its controls passed. It is not evidence of superiority over the starting baseline, which was not run on the held-out tables, and it does not extend the findings beyond the declared cases.\n199:So the re-linearisation, the leverage correction and the polish gave no measured reduction on these cases. The same holds on development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}). A single static ranking by first-order influence followed by exact-refit bisection is therefore enough to reach every budget we report, and the budgets can be viewed as properties of that simple procedure's output rather than of the more elaborate one. Opponent-targeted removal needs more rows in all four cases: 650 versus 62, 6 versus 4, 88 versus 62 and 12 versus 8. The gap is large only for the crowd top pair (10.5 times), and modest elsewhere (1.4 to 1.5 times). Random removal's mean overall score is 0.041, against 0.926 for B2. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n215:\\subsection{Development tables: a three-way tie and an unflipped expert table}\\label{sec:dev}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
