{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvpyMtvx28kEEg5Q32Bv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HsvXGPfycGq8XzcUpmmDKD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, old[:60]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n# conclusion\na=s.index('\\\\section{Conclusion}'); b=s.index('\\\\label{lastbodypage}')\ns=s[:a]+'''\\\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, random deletion rarely helped, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is largely table size and is confounded with participants and era. The expert-judgment development table did not flip within the cap, for an unknown reason. The evidence motivates more tables per design, rank-interval events, exact solvers on small tables to bound the distance to the true minimum, and perturbations beyond deletion.\n\n'''+s[b:]\n# implications\na=s.index('\\\\paragraph{Implications for practice.}'); b=s.index('\\\\section{Conclusion}')\ns=s[:a]+'''\\\\paragraph{Implications for practice.} For auditors, the operational result is that a one-shot influence ranking plus bisection over exact refits certifies small reversal budgets on tables of this size in seconds on a CPU. For leaderboard users, a reported adjacent order in these tables rests on few rows relative to the table; this does not say that the underlying quality difference is small, nor that the order is wrong.\n\n'''+s[b:]\n# intro find: compress\na=s.index('\\\\paragraph{What we find.}'); b=s.index('\\\\paragraph{Contributions.}')\ns=s[:a]+'''\\\\paragraph{What we find.} (i) Budgets are small in all four held-out cases (4 to 62 of 135,630 crowd rows; 8 to 62 of 12,900 NBA rows), and mid pairs are cheaper than top pairs in both tables. (ii) As a share of comparisons the sports budgets are about 10 and 21 times larger, but the two top-pair budgets are the same number of rows (62), so this contrast largely reflects table size and is confounded with participants and era. (iii) A one-shot influence ranking with refit bisection already attains every budget; the additional search machinery produced no measured improvement. (iv) On the six-model expert-judgment development table, no flip was found within the cap, and our evidence cannot say whether that is a property of the data or of the search.\n\n'''+s[b:]\n# threats trim\nrep(' Influence ordering uses a one-step Newton approximation, and its quality could differ on tables with many near-separated participants. Ties count as half a win by evaluator convention; we make no claim about the tie rate of either table. Finally, the development',' Influence ordering uses a one-step Newton approximation. Ties count as half a win by evaluator convention. The development')\nrep('Neither normaliser is privileged: the share of the table is the natural unit for an adversary or a noise process that perturbs a fixed fraction of all votes, whereas the share of a participant\\'s own comparisons is closer to the evidence behind that participant\\'s rating. ','Neither normaliser is privileged: the share of the table suits a perturbation of a fixed fraction of all votes, the share of a participant\\'s comparisons is closer to the evidence behind its rating. ')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/dev/null 2>&1; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/dev/null 2>&1; grep -n \"lastbodypage\\|Conclusion\" paper.aux | head -3; grep -n \"numberline {6}\" paper.aux"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":386,"cache_read_input_tokens":93946,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":386},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35d0884b-633b-4c96-8540-7a451c3c9e1e","uuid":"9e3c36d8-0748-450f-a26b-1b2ced493bf8","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:30:38.528Z","request_id":"req_011CgvpyLycoA7A7zj1rBjfQ"}
