{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ab3q1gpaJhPvx5cw39Rtd4","type":"tool_result","content":"250-\\section{Conclusion}\n251:We measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, random deletion rarely helped, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is largely table size and is confounded with participants and era. The expert-judgment development table did not flip within the cap, for an unknown reason. The evidence motivates more tables per design, rank-interval events, exact solvers on small tables to bound the distance to the true minimum, and perturbations beyond deletion.\\label{lastbodypage}\n83:\\newlabel{lastbodypage}{{7}{10}{Conclusion}{section.7}{}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35d0884b-633b-4c96-8540-7a451c3c9e1e","uuid":"0d7582c1-de88-4d79-abc8-eb68a16e9f4f","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:31:37.157Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"250-\\section{Conclusion}\n251:We measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, random deletion rarely helped, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is largely table size and is confounded with participants and era. The expert-judgment development table did not flip within the cap, for an unknown reason. The evidence motivates more tables per design, rank-interval events, exact solvers on small tables to bound the distance to the true minimum, and perturbations beyond deletion.\\label{lastbodypage}\n83:\\newlabel{lastbodypage}{{7}{10}{Conclusion}{section.7}{}}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
